+
+## 1. Reset order and retained guarantees
+
+At `hive/reducer.py:1095`, `done`, `unlinked` and `new_pair` track acknowledged progress independently of namespace visibility. `established` becomes True only after the intent helper returns (`:1109`). Existing regular markers now receive lstat, open, fstat, file fsync/close and directory fsync/close. A type/readability failure cannot reach confirmation. Journal rename precedes its directory fsync, and the completed-steps list records the acknowledged rename before that fsync. Later partial failures no longer infer an unchanged old pair merely from failed marker inspection.
+
+The retained mutation traces remain 24 / 28 operations and yield **208** interruption cases, **104** caught-error recoveries, **42** leftover/damaged-marker combinations and **72** clearance interruption cases. Another **96** reset retry/live-feed cases pass. Extra I/O coverage remains **48** close/fstat/directory-open injections plus **four** anchor read/open injections. Six successful reset states, seven refusal guards, late replacement and exact joint restore pass. Archives preserve both original files byte-for-byte, replay remains held through two starts, and explicit reissue persists through two more.
+
+The adapted 7734 intent matrix now has fresh/existing traces of **48 / 47 calls**. The added existing-marker fstat boundary accounts for four extra before/after Crash/OSError cases: **380 syscall cases + eight helper-return cases = 388**, versus 384 previously. It checks both fence and audit, acknowledged file-and-directory fsync for any positive durability claim, untouched old pair before confirmation, held live replay, and zero-carry clearance for caught OSError. Four compound fsync/cleanup-denial configurations pass. The only ordinary-matrix expectation adjustment is accepting the new accurate “new pair complete” message where the old text asserted fsync confirmation.
+
+Injected Crash is a caught BaseException boundary model, not an actual process kill or power cut. After-return injections are deliberately conservative: a real syscall may have completed, while the caller has not acknowledged it. `completed_steps` may omit that operation; it must not invent a completed one. No universal filesystem/firmware persistence claim is inferred.
+
+## 2. F-reset-intent-durability-status — CLOSED Medium
+
+The original 7727 zero-fsync and fsync-plus-cleanup-denial witnesses still report not durable and preserve the old pair. The original 7734 classification probes no longer reproduce a positive durability claim without file fsync. The new independent matrix injects EIO/EACCES into `stat` and `lstat`, on the first marker check, confirmation check and every marker call: **12 variants**. `stat` is no longer called; its six controls complete both fsyncs before an injected first-mutation stop. Every lstat fault stops before file synchronization and old-pair mutation. Persistent lstat failure yields `intent_present: null`, a fence `presence_error`, and `reset_in_progress: null`.
+
+Seven additional helper probes exercise regular-file replacement, symlink replacement, directory replacement, fstat EIO, fstat nonregular mode, fstat changed device, and forced O_NOFOLLOW unavailability. On this Linux host, regular replacement fails inode comparison; the symlink swap is refused by O_NOFOLLOW; directory/type/device/fstat faults refuse confirmation. All six enforced controls have no fsync acknowledgment, report not durable and preserve the old pair. The seventh portability control deliberately sets the flag to zero: a symlink to the original inode is then followed and accepted. This is not a Linux regression; do not generalize this review to platforms lacking the flag or to hostile concurrent writers.
+
+Pre-existing directory and dangling-symlink reset-intent controls now fail at classification without file/directory-only “durable” claims. Mode 000 under uid 1001 remains unchanged and undeleted after failed confirmation; fixing permissions then retrying succeeds with held replay. These outcomes match policy `:154`–`:175`.
+
+## 3. Presence reporting, strict checks and remaining gaps
+
+**7734 original metadata cases corrected.** Failure reading the old anchor after journal rename plus marker-lstat EIO reports unknown presence and the acknowledged rename, not an unchanged pair. Failed final unlink plus marker-lstat EIO reports a complete new pair and unknown removal, not a successful unlink. Both continue to fence live and on ordinary restart. Six additional failures immediately after intent write, journal rename, old-anchor copy, new-anchor write, new-journal replacement and final unlink check null presence, accurate progress and consistent fence/audit completed-step arrays.
+
+**Residual Low:** `presence_error` is attached to the failure fence at `hive/reducer.py:1207`, but omitted from the appended failed audit dictionary at `:1208`–`:1212`. All six added cases reproduce this. The audit's `error` contains only the primary exception, so the secondary namespace-inspection error is unavailable to audit-only consumers. The requested “null + presence_error” diagnostic is therefore only partly implemented. Add that field to the audit too, or explicitly narrow policy `:163`–`:167` and the public schema. This is not a replay failure.
+
+**Whole-hive audit.** AST inspection finds zero calls to `os.path.lexists/isfile/islink/exists`, `Path.exists/is_file/is_dir`, or `os.access`; the literal names remaining in reducer `:450` are documentation. `FileNotFoundError` at the strict lstat helper means native ENOENT; EIO, EACCES and ENOTDIR propagate. Cleanup-only broad OSError handlers remove temporary files best-effort and re-raise the original error. Runtime verification errors fence/raise, and append-close logging occurs after fsync, not as an absence decision. Other modules' broad exception handlers log polling/planning/adapter failures and do not decide journal namespace presence.
+
+The independent strict-check matrix records **45 combinations**: startup/reset/clear × intent/journal/anchor/two legacy fence paths × EIO/EACCES/ENOTDIR. **42 apply and pass**; clearance's three anchor-read cases are correctly not applicable because clearance replaces the old anchor without reading it. `.id` is checked by open/read, not lstat; clear similarly reads journal content directly. Startup intent errors are treated present, drop pending decisions, fence `reset_incomplete` and expose null `reset_in_progress`; other startup faults fence `unwritable`. Applicable reset/clear faults raise and stay fenced; live replay remains held.
+
+**New Medium: dangling anchor is not absence of its directory entry.** `_read_anchor()` still uses `open(self._id_path(path), "rb")` and catches FileNotFoundError (`:531`–`:534`) without checking the entry itself. An anchor symlink with a missing referent produces ENOENT even though the `.id` entry exists. With the journal missing, `_open_journal()` concludes neither exists (`:669`) and creates a journal (`:670`), replacing the symlink through `_write_anchor()` rather than fencing. `focused7741.json → dangling_anchor` creates this real fixture and records healthy=True, a newly created journal and anchor_replaced=True. No monkeypatched OS failure is needed.
+
+This is a **pre-existing** hole exposed by this round's broadened strict-presence audit, not introduced by the new lstat helper. It violates the startup's documented read-only behavior for damaged artifacts and “new only when neither exists” rule (policy `:105`–`:111`; reducer `:630`–`:638`), and the new universal strict-entry claim (policy `:160`; README `:109`). The effect is silent loss of an existing recovery/identity entry and healthy admission of ambiguous storage, **not** replay of a discarded authorization. Require explicit error-propagating namespace inspection when anchor open reports ENOENT: only a genuinely absent anchor entry is first-use; an existing dangling/nonreadable entry must fence and remain untouched. Rerun regular/missing/dangling anchor controls and first-use/clearance matrices. The Medium artifact-preservation violation is the production-gate blocker; the Low audit-field omission alone would not establish a storage replay defect.
+
+## 4. Retained integrity checks and accepted limitations
+
+All **16 primary / eight focused journal groups** pass. **1,440 supersession schedules / 15,840 event-prefix checks** and **144 hold/rebind schedules / 1,152 checks** pass. Applicable 7638–7734 content/order, refusal, pending/commit failures, identity adoption, legacy startup, unreadable-file, artifact and valid-prefix controls are rerun via their retained composite harnesses. Historical `recovery7708.restore_guidance` retains its obsolete clearance-only witness; successful reset corrects that recovery path.
+
+These architectural limits are accurately documented and are not gate blockers:
+
+- Unkeyed hashes are not a MAC; a writer can recompute them (policy `:190`–`:193`).
+- Joint journal/`.id` rollback without successful pre-replay reset can revive discarded decisions (policy `:112`–`:132`; README `:100`–`:104`).
+- Clean complete-record suffix truncation with matching identity is accepted at restart and can drop rebinds; live whole-file verification may detect it (policy `:183`–`:190`; README `:100`–`:101`).
+- Snapshot, partial and synthetic replay are unsupported; recover using the full original ordered stream (policy `:87`–`:89`, `:127`–`:128`).
+- If intent cannot be confirmed durable, the old pair is intact before confirmation; a visible partial marker may still be lost. Keep service stopped and retry before replay (policy `:168`–`:175`; README `:107`–`:108`).
+- Symlink/directory reset markers require stopped-service operator cleanup and retry; unreadable regular markers require permission repair, not deletion (policy `:157`–`:159`, `:174`–`:175`).
+
+Both files genuinely absent remains indistinguishable from first use. Protect storage permissions, keep one writer, retain the original ordered event stream, and perform recovery before serving/replay. Synchronous verification is linear in journal size; performance qualification, hardware power-cut behavior and no-O_NOFOLLOW platforms remain outside scope. These accepted limits do not excuse the observed existing-anchor-entry overwrite. Approval is withheld until the Medium startup gap is corrected and independently witnessed closed; complete the Low audit field in the same repair.
+
+## 5. Suite, execution accounting and publication integrity
+
+Full suite executed **exactly once**: **840 passed in 50.37 s**, exit 0, runner wall **50.9045 s**. Sixteen additional tests relative to 7734 account for the increase from 824. All **161 tracked non-experiment files** match pinned Git blobs, initial export and repository source. Prior manifests verified **315 / 351 / 355 / 829 / 1,189 / 2,221 / 3,913 / 5,821** artifact hashes for 7669/7678/7694/7701/7708/7718/7727/7734.
+
+All execution output is retained without overwriting logs. The sole nonzero substantive run is `semantic-7133`, repeating three already-documented obsolete automatic-closure expectations; its 3,072 current-format, 3,072 prior-format, 384 legacy and lifecycle controls pass. Previously corrected `probes-final`, `older-regressions-final` and `independent-final` scripts pass directly. The suite was not rerun. `audit7741-final` corrects only the first audit's mistaken line-reference note; both originals are retained. Preliminary shell status inspection while the suite was still starting found no exit file yet and made no changes.
+
+Each substantive run has exclusive started/exit JSON and stdout/stderr. Commands, environment allowlist, local-wheel hashes, copied-script provenance/adaptation diffs, source patch and changed-file snapshots are retained. Python sockets are guarded and builds use existing local wheels; this is not an OS network sandbox. Setup exports source without running tests. Copied legacy dependency scripts not directly executed are distinguished by the execution ledger, not assumed to have standalone results.
+
+The report/evidence secret scan found **zero credential-pattern matches**; its exact scope and limitations are retained. No environment dump or credential value was captured. Only this report and `experiments/20261008-astra-7741/` were written in the repository. `SHA256SUMS` covers every delivered regular evidence file and this report, excluding itself; read-only `verify_evidence.py` checks complete coverage, hashes, execution ledgers, suite count, regression matrices and findings. No production/test/scratch edit, staging, commit or push was performed. Parent publication remains separate.
